// time module: wall clock, monotonic clock, sleep.
//
// C implementation: lib/time.c (lazily dlopen'd on first call).
//
//   now_ms()       wall-clock (CLOCK_REALTIME) milliseconds
//   monotonic_ms() CLOCK_MONOTONIC milliseconds, same source as vm.time_ms
//   sleep(ms)      blocks ~ms milliseconds, returns 0; non-int arg -> -1
//                  (the C side guards the arg: the param below is
//                  deliberately untyped — a lowercase `float`-style
//                  annotation currently quantifies as a type variable at
//                  the type layer, language changes are frozen this phase)
//
// sleep SUSPENSION MECHANISM (v1): blocking nanosleep inside the C call.
// This BLOCKS THE WORKER THREAD for the duration — other actors on the
// same worker stall, and each concurrent sleeper pins one worker. It was
// chosen because no existing scheduler mechanism is a faithful sleep:
// recv_after(ms) does not block a worker but is mailbox-bound (a message
// arriving mid-wait wakes the caller early AND consumes the message),
// and a mailbox-independent wait-deadline would require a scheduler
// change (out of scope). Full rationale: lib/time.c header comment.
// Callers needing parallel sleeps: run with NWORKERS >= sleeper count,
// or wait with recv_after when mailbox semantics are acceptable.

external fn time.now_ms() -> int
external fn time.monotonic_ms() -> int
external fn time.sleep(ms) -> int
